# Generated files, of two kinds: Python's own bytecode cache, and nightshift's runtime
# output. Everything else nightshift writes is committed on purpose — the manifest, the
# gates, the board, the corrections log and the install receipt are all properties of
# the repo. What is listed below is not.
#
# Comments live on their own line: a `#` after a pattern is part of the pattern.

# Python bytecode cache. Standard for every Python project, and its absence here
# let a worker's own `pytest` run leave `__pycache__/` behind uncommitted; the
# runner's wip-banking `git add -A` then tracked it (nothing here told it not to),
# and a later worktree cut had to materialise the tracked cache files.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd

# Per-run worker logs and transcripts. Large, and meaningful only on the machine
# that produced them.
.ai/runs/

# The preflight receipt. Names a commit SHA on one machine; a committed one would
# unblock a push somebody else never checked.
.ai/.preflight

# Per-machine dispatch config, resolved from the committed .ai/hosts.json.
.ai/host.json

# The stop file. Its presence halts the runner, so it must never arrive by pull.
.ai/STOP

# Which harvested audio candidate was picked for each sound. Per-listener,
# machine-local state the Command Center's audio-candidates view writes — the
# durable record is whatever adoption step a project defines once a pick is
# acted on, not this file.
.ai/audio_picks.json

# `nightshift update`'s working files. `--take` keeps what it replaced as `-old` so an
# overwrite is recoverable; `--merge` hands the agent the template as `-new` and removes
# it afterwards. Both are per-machine scratch on the way to a decision, and a committed
# one is a copy of a file that also exists properly, three directories away.
*.nightshift-old
*.nightshift-new

# Editor state, per machine and none of git's business. Named here rather than left
# to each operator because the failure it prevents is specific and silent: an editor
# rewrites its own state on almost every interaction, including just looking at a
# file, so a tracked one is dirty again seconds after you commit it -- and the runner
# refuses to dispatch on a dirty tree. Opening the board to read it is then what
# stops the board being worked.
#
# The directory wholesale, unlike the two volatile files this used to name. The narrow
# form existed because `init` wrote real shared config into `.obsidian/` (the plugin
# lists for the board view); nothing does any more -- Command Center is the board
# surface -- so the whole directory is per-machine and none of it should be committed.
.obsidian/
.vscode/
.idea/
